From 11affe6e73579463bec60b8b5ad92df6a656dfe2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ethan Lee <ethantlee21@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 19:40:19 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] feat: make the session daemon its own responsible process on
 macOS
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

macOS attributes privacy-gated operations (Local Network, TCC) to a
process's *responsible process*, a value fixed at spawn and inherited from
the parent. A session created by `zmx attach` was therefore attributed to
the terminal app that ran it, and the moment that app exited every process
in the session became responsible for itself; a client reattaching over
the socket never re-attributes them, since reattach is a connect, not a
fork. From then on each program in the session was judged by its own
identity, which for anything that is not an Apple binary meant local
network access refused with EHOSTUNREACH and no prompt (macterm#419).

Responsibility cannot be changed after the fact (the responsibility_set_*
calls need a private entitlement), but the disclaim exists as a spawn
attribute, and POSIX_SPAWN_SETEXEC lets the forked child apply it to
itself by exec'ing in place: same pid, same session, same fds. The daemon
now re-execs as `zmx __daemon <session> [-- command...]` with the rest of
its state in ZMX_DAEMON_* variables that the entry consumes before the
shell exists, and picks up where run() left off. The listen socket is
inherited (its CLOEXEC cleared) rather than recreated, because the client
connects the moment the fork returns. If the re-exec fails the daemon
carries on in the forked image, exactly as before.

The daemon is then the responsible process for as long as the session
lives, whatever happens to the app, and its own identity is what the
system asks the user about — so the binary embeds an Info.plist
(__TEXT,__info_plist) naming com.thdxg.macterm.zmx with a Local Network
usage description; Macterm signs its bundled copy with the app's release
certificate so that identity is stable across updates.

Linux is unchanged. The new bats file pins the contract on Darwin and
skips elsewhere.
